<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Before you start</h2>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>A VoiceML account with a phone number.</strong> See <a href="/voiceml/">VoiceML</a> or <a href="/signup/">start a free trial</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>The number that should ring during open hours</strong>, your office&rsquo;s time zone as an IANA name such as <code>America/Chicago</code>, and your holiday dates.</li>
	</ol>
	<div class="alert alert-warning" role="note">
		<p><strong>The reception number in this document is fictional.</strong> It uses the 555-0100 to 555-0199 range, which is set aside for fiction. Replace it with a real number before you publish.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 1: Publish the document and point the number at it</h2>
	<p>Change the time zone, hours, holidays, and reception number to your own. Then publish it at <a href="{{ site.voicemlPublisherUrl }}">voiceml.org</a> and set the <code>/r/</code> URL it returns as the phone number&rsquo;s voice URL. Keep the URL exactly as returned, <code>key</code> included. The key is a shared secret, so don&rsquo;t post the URL publicly.</p>
	<p class="code-block-header">main.xml</p>
	<pre class="code-block" data-lang="xml"><code>{{ voicemlSamples["office-hours"].main | escape }}</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 2: Test it</h2>
	<p>Call during your open hours and reception rings. To hear the closed message without waiting for the evening, change <code>tz</code> to a time zone where the office would be closed right now, such as <code>Asia/Tokyo</code> during a US workday, publish that copy, and point the number at it. Point the number back at the real document when you&rsquo;re done.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>How the schedule works</h2>
	<p><code>{% raw %}"now" | date: "%H%M", tz{% endraw %}</code> formats the moment the call arrives as hours and minutes in the office&rsquo;s time zone, such as <code>0930</code>, and <code>{% raw %}| plus: 0{% endraw %}</code> turns it into the number 930, so it can be compared with the opening and closing times. The document reads four values this way:</p>
	<div class="table-container">
		<table class="data-table">
			<caption>The date values the document reads</caption>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th scope="col">Variable</th>
					<th scope="col">Format</th>
					<th scope="col">Example</th>
					<th scope="col">Used for</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>today</code></td><td><code>%Y-%m-%d</code></td><td><code>2026-11-26</code></td><td>Holidays that move from year to year</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>monthday</code></td><td><code>%m-%d</code></td><td><code>12-25</code></td><td>Holidays on the same date every year, and the early close</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>weekday</code></td><td><code>%u</code></td><td><code>6</code></td><td>Weekends: 1 is Monday and 7 is Sunday</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>time</code></td><td><code>%H%M</code></td><td><code>930</code></td><td>Opening and closing times</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>Two <code>{% raw %}{% case %}{% endraw %}</code> blocks set <code>holiday</code> to the holiday&rsquo;s name when today matches a date. The checks then run in order, and the first that applies decides the call: a holiday, then a weekend, then a time outside the opening hours. Only a call that passes all three rings reception. In 2026, Independence Day falls on a Saturday, so the office closes on Friday, July 3, which is listed as its own date.</p>
</section>

